zation: Use OpenMP simd on inner j loop, but also unroll manually? Or use #pragma omp simd reduction(max:mx) with aligned arrays. Also maybe use int32 for P if we can guarantee no overflow by checking max element? But not safe.\n\nWe could compute prefix sums using long long and then query; the inner loop over j is independent, so SIMD works well. We'll also parallelize over i with OpenMP reduction(max:mx). This is essentially what previous experiment likely did. To differentiate, we can add #pragma omp simd safelen(8) or use `#pragma GCC ivdep` to tell compiler no dependencies.\n\nMaybe the improvement is to avoid computing prefix sums for all rows if S is large? Not needed.\n\nAnother angle: The problem allows modifying any way; maybe we can use a different algorithm like Kadane's 2D but with fixed size? No, need exact max of fixed-size submatrix.\n\nGiven role B specifically \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\", perhaps the expected transformation is to add OpenMP simd directives to the original sliding window inner loops (which are reductions) and ensure no aliasing by using restrict pointers. That was already done in e28e353d75544686, but maybe we can improve it further by also parallelizing outer while loop? The while loop has dependencies due to sum updates; cannot parallelize easily.\n\nMaybe we can transform the original sliding window into a version where inner loops over k are vectorized with `#pragma omp simd reduction(+:sum)` and use `__restrict` on matrix pointer. Also unroll by 4? But previous experiment already did that and got same time as prefix sum, suggesting memory bandwidth bound.\n\nLet's look at the current source (original) again. It uses vector<vector<int>> which is not cache friendly due to indirection. We could change to flat array `int* matrix = new int[n*n]` or std::vector<int> with row-major indexing. That alone might improve cache and allow SIMD better. Then add OpenMP simd on inner k loops.\n\nBut previous experiment e28e353d75544686 likely already did that? Its hypothesis mentions \"restrict qualifiers\" so probably used flat arrays.\n\nWe need propose a new candidate, not repeat exactly. Perhaps we can combine prefix sum with manual vectorization using intrinsics? But compiler may auto-vectorize with -O3 and OpenMP simd.\n\nGiven the task says \"You propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment.\" We should choose a transformation that is likely to improve over current best (0.035s). Since we don't have actual code of previous experiments, but we can infer they used prefix sum + parallel reduction. To beat them, maybe use a more cache-blocked approach: process the query loop in tiles so that P rows are reused across j and i.\n\nFor example, for each block of rows (say 64), compute all submatrix sums within that block using sliding window horizontally? Actually with 2D prefix, we can iterate over i blocks and j blocks to improve locality. But compiler may already do well.\n\nAnother idea: Use int32 for P if possible by storing differences? Not safe.\n\nMaybe use `#pragma omp simd` on the inner loop but also add `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)` with chunk size 1 to balance load, and use reduction(max) across threads. Also ensure alignment using `__attribute__((aligned(64)))`.\n\nLet's craft a concrete implementation:\n\n- Read n,s.\n- Allocate flat vector<long long> P((n+1)*(n+1),0).\n- Fill P: for i=1..n, rowSum=0; for j=1..n: rowSum += mat[i*n+j]? Actually we need read matrix first. We can compute prefix on the fly while reading? Input is whitespace separated ints; we can read into flat vector<int> mat(n*n) then compute P.\n\n- Compute P:\n   for i=0;i<n;i++){\n       long long rowSum=0;\n       for j=0;j<n;j++){\n           rowSum += mat[i*n+j];\n           P[(i+1)*(n+1)+(j+1)] = P[i*(n+1)+(j+1)] + rowSum; // Actually standard: P[i+1][j+1] = P[i][j+1] + P[i+1][j] - P[i][j] + mat[i][j]\n       }\n   }\n\nBetter use the formula.\n\n- Then query:\n   long long mx = LLONG_MIN;\n   #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mx) schedule(static)\n   for(int i=0;i<=n-s;i++){\n       const long long* rowTop = &P[i*(n+1)];\n       const long long* rowBot = &P[(i+s)*(n+1)];\n       long long localMax = LLONG_MIN;\n       #pragma omp simd reduction(max:localMax)\n       for(int j=0;j<=n-s;j++){\n           long long sum = rowBot[j+s] - rowTop[j+s] - (rowBot[j] - rowTop[j]); // Actually P[i+S][j+S] - P[i][j+S] - P[i+S][j] + P[i][j]\n           // Let's compute: topLeft = i, j; bottomRight = i+S,j+S\n           long long val = rowBot[j+s] - rowTop[j+s] - (rowBot[j] - rowTop[j]); Wait that equals? \n           // Standard: sum = P[i+S][j+S] - P[i][j+S] - P[i+S][j] + P[i][j]\n           // With row pointers: top = P[i], bot = P[i+S]; then val = bot[j+s] - top[j+s] - (bot[j] - top[j])? Actually bot[j+s]-top[j+s] gives sum of rows i..i+S-1, cols 0..j+s-1. Then subtract bot[j]-top[j] which is rows i..i+S-1, cols 0..j-1. So val = (bot[j+s]-top[j+s]) - (bot[j]-top[j]). That's correct.\n           localMax = std::max(localMax, val);\n       }\n   }\n\nBut note: The inner loop uses rowBot and rowTop pointers; these are independent across j, so SIMD can vectorize. However the reduction(max) on localMax is fine.\n\nWe also need to ensure that `#pragma omp simd` doesn't conflict with OpenMP parallel for; it's allowed inside.\n\nPotential issue: The inner loop has two loads from rowBot and rowTop at indices j+s and j, which may cause gather? But contiguous access if we iterate j sequentially; compiler can vectorize using unaligned loads. We could also precompute arrays diff[j] = bot[j]-top[j], then sum = diff[j+s] - diff[j]; but that's extra memory.\n\nMaybe better to compute for each i a temporary array `colDiff` of length n+1: colDiff[k] = P[i+S][k] - P[i][k]. Then subSum(i,j) = colDiff[j+S] - colDiff[j]. This reduces loads from 4 to 2 per query. We can compute colDiff once per i (O(N)) and then vectorize over j with two loads. That might be faster.\n\nImplementation:\n\nfor i in parallel:\n   long long* top = &P[i*(n+1)];\n   long long* bot = &P[(i+s)*(n+1)];\n   // compute diff array\n   for k=0..n: diff[k] = bot[k]-top[k];\n   #pragma omp simd reduction(max:localMax)\n   for j=0..n-s:\n       localMax = max(localMax, diff[j+s]-diff[j]);\n\nThis adds O(N) per i but reduces inner loop memory traffic. Total extra O(N^2) operations (subtractions), same order.\n\nWe can also use `#pragma omp simd` on the diff computation? Not needed.\n\nGiven N small, this should be fast.\n\nLet's write full code:\n\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <climits>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint main() {\n    int n,s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    std::vector<int> mat(n*n);\n    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++) \n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i*n+j]);\n\n    // 2D prefix sums, size (n+1)x(n+1)\n    int W = n+1;\n    std::vector<long long> P(W*W, 0);\n    for(int i=0;i<n;i++){\n        long long rowSum = 0;\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++){\n            rowSum += mat[i*n+j];\n            // P[(i+1)*W + (j+1)] = P[i*W + (j+1)] + rowSum; // This is wrong? Let's derive.\n            // Standard: P[i+1][j+1] = P[i][j+1] + P[i+1][j] - P[i][j] + mat[i][j]\n            // We can compute using previous row and current row cumulative.\n        }\n    }\n\nBetter use the standard formula:\n\nfor(int i=0;i<n;i++){\n    for(int j=0;j<n;j++){\n        P[(i+1)*W + (j+1)] = P[i*W + (j+1)] + P[(i+1)*W + j] - P[i*W + j] + mat[i*n+j];\n    }\n}\n\nThis is O(N^2) with 4 loads/stores.\n\nThen query:\n\nlong long mx = LLONG_MIN;\nstd::vector<long long> diff(n+1);\n#pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mx) schedule(static)\nfor(int i=0;i<=n-s;i++){\n    const long long* top = &P[i*W];\n    const long long* bot = &P[(i+s)*W];\n    // compute diff\n    #pragma omp simd\n    for(int k=0;k<=n;k++) diff[k] = bot[k]-top[k]; // But diff is shared across threads -> race! Need per-thread local array.\n}\n\nAh, diff must be private to each thread. We can declare it inside the parallel loop body (each iteration has its own). Since #pragma omp parallel for creates a team of threads; variables declared outside are shared by default unless specified firstprivate. Better to allocate diff inside the loop:\n\n#pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mx) schedule(static)\nfor(int i=0;i<=n-s;i++){\n    std::vector<long long> localDiff(n+1);\n    const long long* top = &P[i*W];\n    const long long* bot = &P[(i+s)*W];\n    #pragma omp simd\n    for(int k=0;k<=n;k++) localDiff[k] = bot[k]-top[k];\n    long long localMax = LLONG_MIN;\n    #pragma omp simd reduction(max:localMax)\n    for(int j=0;j<=n-s;j++){\n        long long sum = localDiff[j+s] - localDiff[j];\n        if(sum>localMax) localMax=sum;\n    }\n    mx = std::max(mx, localMax);\n}\n\nBut allocating vector each iteration may be costly. We can use a fixed-size array with dynamic allocation outside and make it private via firstprivate? Actually we can declare `long long* diff = new long long[n+1];` inside the loop; but that's heap alloc per i (up to 8000 times) -> overhead. Better allocate once as thread-local using OpenMP threadprivate or just use a local array on stack if n small (n<8000, so ~64KB stack per thread maybe okay). We can declare `long long diff[n+1];` inside the loop; variable-length arrays are not standard C++ but GCC supports. Or use std::vector with reserve outside and copy? Simpler: Use a local array of size n+1 on stack; N<8000 so 64KB, acceptable.\n\nBut note: The inner query loop uses diff[j+s] - diff[j]; this has two loads from same array at offsets s apart. SIMD can vectorize if we use unaligned loads; compiler may do it.\n\nAlternatively, avoid diff and directly compute using top/bot pointers with 4 loads per j; that might be fine too.\n\nLet's consider performance: The main cost is memory bandwidth for P (long long). Each query reads 4 longs = 32 bytes. For N=1000,S=500, queries ~250k -> 8MB, trivial. So likely compute-bound not memory-bound.\n\nMaybe the previous experiment already did this and got 0.035s; to beat it we need something else.\n\nCould use int32 for P if we can prove no overflow by using `__int128` accumulation? Actually we could store prefix sums as int64 but maybe use int32 with careful modulo? No, exact sum needed.\n\nAnother idea: Use the fact that S is fixed and N<8000; we can compute all submatrix sums using a 2D convolution with a box filter of size SxS. That's O(N^2) using integral image (prefix sum). Same as above.\n\nMaybe use SIMD to speed up prefix computation itself? The prefix loop has dependencies across i and j, but we can parallelize over rows? Actually P[i+1][j+1] depends on P[i][j+1], P[i+1][j], P[i][j]; there is a wavefront dependency. We could use OpenMP to compute anti-diagonals in parallel, but that's complex and may not help.\n\nGiven the task expects one candidate; we can propose an implementation with flat arrays, 2D prefix sums using long long, OpenMP parallel for over i with reduction(max), and inside each row use `#pragma omp simd` on j loop computing sum from diff array. Also add `__restrict__` or restrict qualifiers to help compiler.\n\nLet's write the code carefully.\n\nWe need ensure correctness: The output is a single integer (sum fits 32-bit). We'll print as long long? printf(\"%lld\\n\", mx) but expected int; use %d with cast if safe. Since result guaranteed fit in 32-bit, we can cast to int and print %d.\n\nLet's implement:\n\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <climits>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint main() {\n    int n,s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    std::vector<int> mat(n*n);\n    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++) \n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i*n+j]);\n\n    const int W = n+1;\n    std::vector<long long> P(W*W, 0LL);\n\n    // Compute 2D prefix sums\n    for(int i=0;i<n;i++){\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++){\n            P[(i+1)*W + (j+1)] = P[i*W + (j+1)] + P[(i+1)*